	abstract = {Infections at the bone-implant interface remain a major limitation of osseointegrated bionic limb prostheses, often leading to prolonged treatment, additional surgeries, or implant failure. Electrical stimulation has emerged as a promising adjunctive strategy to prevent bacterial colonization and mitigate biofilm formation, provided that stimulation is delivered in a safe and controlled manner suitable for longterm use. This work presents the design rationale and requirements analysis of a research platform intended to deliver alternating, charge-balanced electrical stimulation through an existing osseointegrated prosthetic interface. The proposed system is designed for autonomous, long-duration therapy delivery during periods when the prosthesis is not worn, while supporting supervised clinical interaction for configuration, monitoring, and data retrieval. A structured requirements engineering process was followed, informed by clinical use cases, existing prosthetic system constraints, and applicable medical device standards. Key functional, safety, and usability requirements are identified, and a modular hardware and embedded software architecture is proposed to isolate safety-critical stimulation functions from communication and telemetry tasks. Preliminary experimental results obtained on the prototype validate core system functionality. This paper represents a work in progress, focusing on system requirements and architectural design of the research platform. Experimental validation and clinical evaluation will be addressed in future work. © 2026 IEEE.},
