	abstract = {Ambiguity in software requirements specifications (SRS) relics a tireless trial that can result in heavy cost, delays in project, quality challenges and failure of projects. SRS is normally designed by applying traditional methods and "natural language (NL)". NL is syntactically vague and semantically unreliable. Inaccurate, improper and incomplete requirement cause project failure and also create ambiguity in SRS. Formal Methods are used to reduce ambiguity and define the system precisely. This paper presents a framework for reducing ambiguity in SRS incorporating formal methods. By translating vague requirements into precise mathematical definitions and employing techniques framework ensures clearer, more consistent requirements. Case studies show it enhances requirement precision, stakeholder satisfaction, and reduces development risks, providing a robust solution for more reliable SRS. The framework is evaluated by comparing it to traditional SRS methods through a published case studies and expert reviews from industry professionals with over 12 years of experience in various software development, business and academic roles. It outperforms other existing studies by achieving 93% reduction in SRS ambiguity.  © 2025 IEEE.},
