	affiliations = {China Aerospace Academy of Systems Science and Engineering, Beijing, China},
	abstract = {As the complexity and diversity of requirements continue to increase, traditional manual-based requirement analysis methods are increasingly unable to meet the efficiency and precision demands of modern aerospace system design. Requirement knowledge graphs, as an emerging technical approach, offer a novel solution for aerospace requirements engineering. Against this backdrop, we propose a method for constructing aerospace requirement knowledge graphs, which involves data acquisition, ontology definition, 'entityrelationship' extraction, and graph construction. To address current shortcomings in identifying key entities and relationships in unstructured requirements during knowledge graph construction, we introduce an improved EfeN-CasRel-MOA 'entity-relationship' joint extraction model. This model first enhances the potential correlation of head entity position features in the text through an EfeNet module and incorporates a MOA mechanism. By extracting text features at multiple scales, the model enhances its ability to capture both local details and global semantics, while the orthogonal attention mechanism further improves the prediction of tail entities and relationships. The model is trained, validated, and tested using a constructed aerospace requirement text dataset, with comprehensive evaluations conducted. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed model outperforms existing comparison models. Finally, using the extraction results from the proposed model, an aerospace requirement knowledge graph is constructed, which preliminarily achieves the expected goals of requirement analysis and management, providing robust support for subsequent structured requirement generation.  © 2026 IEEE.},
