	affiliations = {VTU Extension Centre, Honeywell Technology Solutions, Bangalore, India; Manipal Institute of Technology, Manipal, India},
	abstract = {In the safety-critical systems domain, Model-Based Development (MBD) is an increasingly popular approach for development of digital control systems. In this approach, system engineers develop and simulate system models composed of hardware and software requirements using MBD tools like Simulink, Stateflow or SCADE etc. In recent years, formal methods are gaining popularity in the safety and security critical reliable systems. This article deals with a deeper understanding of application of formal methods. The formal methods ensure that the implementation of a software product should satisfy the requirements specification. They help in identifying ambiguous requirements and expose flaws in the system and software requirements. They help in writing disambiguate system specifications and articulate implicate assumptions. This article includes a design study of formal methods for autopilot mode transition and voter logic validation using model checking. The autopilot mode transition and voter logic is implemented using Simulink and S-functions. The models developed in Simulink and S-function are verified formally using the model checking tool, Simulink Design Verifier. The analysis of Simulink Design Verifier results and its benefits is discussed in this article. © 2021, The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Singapore Pte Ltd.},
