	affiliations = {Monash Univ, Melbourne},
	abstract = {This paper explores the potential of using multiple software requirements specification methods for elicitation of concepts in the development of a large hypermedia system. One of the characteristics of the business reengineering process is its greater demand for improved interpersonal communications. At present, the use of hypermedia and multimedia technology is perceived as one of the most efficient ways to enhance people's access to the information and improve communication in business. The process which plays the critical role in such a development environment is the concepts acquisition and requirements specification. The hypermedia design and programming development process shows some peculiarities which make it different from the 'classic' software development activities. The main area of interest is requirements engineering, modelling of multimedia temporal relations, and removing ambiguity of natural language from software requirements specifications. There is a need to devise software requirements specification notations which would cater for the irregularities introduced by the differences in media while supporting commonly used development activities. In this paper, we assume a typical hypermedia development process in a business environment and contrast specific differences and problems which differentiate hypermedia based projects from other commercial projects. The objective is to analyse the role of a life-cycle model and key activities of the requirements management to show that the use of multiple formal methods for the user requirements collection in the software development process is inevitable in order to achieve a more efficient utilisation of complex computing technologies.},
	keywords = {Data acquisition; Information retrieval systems; Interactive computer systems; Management information systems; Software engineering; Business process reengineering; Large hypermedia system; Multimedia information systems; Administrative data processing},
