	affiliations = {Inst Informatiki Univ, Lyudviga-Maksimiliana, Myunkhen, Germany},
	abstract = {Requirements engineering describes the process leading in a systematic way from the informal objectives of a software development problem to a requirements specification. This specification serves as a contract for the consecutive software development. In most current requirement engineering methods requirement specifications consist of informal and pre-formal descriptions such as plain text and diagrams describing different views of the required system, including data structures, control and dataflow. Due to the different nature of the employed description methods it is often difficult to get a comprehensive view of all requirements. In this paper the algebraic-axiomatic approach to software specification is proposed as an integrating formal model for requirement descriptions. It is argued that formal specification techniques are complementary to informal ones and that they can safely be integrated in an iterative requirement development process. Three results from the German National Project KORSO are presented: It is shown how entity relationship diagrams, dataflow diagrams and access rights can be modeled by algebraic specifications in the language SPECTRUM. These schematic techniques are illustrated by a small part of a KORSO case study on the requirement specification for a patient information system of a hospital.},
