	abstract = {Requirements specifications are typically generated by the requirements analyst after consulting with the user. There are several ways to gather and analyze requirements, but the basic modeling technique for all these approaches is the dataflow model, which describes the system in terms of information and its flow. VArious techniques can then be applied to pave the way for high-level design. The most popular are structured analysis, which effectively partitions system requirements, and the more recent object-oriented analysis, which describes the system in terms of objects, their data attributes and operations, as well as inter-object communication, object relations, and assembly structures. Another promising method is to analyze scenarios - possible ways to use the system to accomplish some function the user desires. Scenarios offer promise as a way to tame requirements analysis, bur progress has been impeded by the lack of a systematic way to analyze, generate, and validate them. The authors propose such a method and apply it to a simple PBX system. Their method has a formal mathematical base, generates precise scenarios, accommodates change, and keeps users involved in the process.},
